<!--
  Bound ClickHouse's self-logging (#96a). Left at defaults, the system.* introspection
  tables grow without TTL and dwarf the actual telemetry: measured 2026-07-03 on the
  lab stack, system.* held 5.84 GiB (text_log alone 2.95 GiB) against 453 MiB of
  netops data, on a host at 82% disk.

  Policy:
    * DISABLED  — logs that only matter during a deep server-side investigation.
      Re-enable one temporarily by deleting its <remove> line here and
      `docker compose restart clickhouse`; it starts logging again immediately.
    * KEPT+TTL  — query_log (slow-query debugging, 7d), error_log (the only
      record of background-thread errors, 7d), metric_log (ad-hoc perf
      history, 3d), part_log (merge/part accounting, 3d),
      asynchronous_metric_log (RSS / jemalloc / cache history, 3d) and
      trace_log (memory profiler sink, 1d).

  EVERY kept log carries a bounded <ttl> AND a merge-safe <settings> block. A
  system log without a TTL is the #96a regression (5.84 GiB of self-logging vs
  453 MiB of data); a system log without the merge settings is the P2/s06
  regression documented below. The contract tests
  tests/test_clickhouse_merge_budget.py and
  tests/test_clickhouse_system_log_merges.py fail if either is lost.

  part_log RE-ENABLED 2026-08-29 (docs/scale/P2_CLICKHOUSE_MEMFLAT_2026-08-29.md
  section 1). The P2 merge-storm investigation had to RECONSTRUCT the merge
  counts from metric_log + query_log because part_log was off: 69,201 merges and
  337.6 GiB merged in 75 min were ESTIMATED, not measured, and the per-table
  merge write amplification (~241x) could only be inferred. part_log is the one
  table that answers "which table, which part, how big, how long" directly, and
  the merge-budget settings shipped alongside it (memory.xml,
  init.sql/corr_merge_budget.go) can only be VERIFIED against it.
  Cost control: 3-day TTL, and the merge volume it records is exactly what the
  new budget bounds — at the pre-fix rate that is ~1.3M small rows/day.

  ==========================================================================
  P2/s06 — WHY EVERY KEPT LOG NOW CARRIES <settings>
  (docs/scale/P2_CLICKHOUSE_PEAK_S06_2026-08-29.md)

  MEASURED, 14:35-14:50 on 2026-08-29: `system.metric_log` (997 columns,
  1,015 B/row, enabled the same day with the 3-day TTL below) produced 13
  one-second RSS transients up to 4,406 MiB = 107 % of max_server_memory_usage,
  and 17 MEMORY_LIMIT_EXCEEDED refusals of unrelated inserts, on a ~46 s
  cadence that matches `ProfileEvent_MergedColumns = 997` exactly — the
  metric_log merge completing. Its own merges are INVISIBLE in part_log
  (see "part_log cannot see this" below), which is why the incident first
  read as a contradiction.

  THE MECHANISM (verified against the ClickHouse v24.8.14.39-lts source, not
  inferred):

    * MergeTreeData::choosePartFormat  (MergeTreeData.cpp)
        Compact  iff  bytes_uncompressed < min_bytes_for_wide_part
                  OR  rows_count        < min_rows_for_wide_part
      Stock: 10,485,760 B / 0 rows  ->  Wide above ~10,330 metric_log rows.
    * MergeTask::chooseMergeAlgorithm  (MergeTask.cpp:1304)
        Horizontal unless the OUTPUT part is Wide AND
          rows    >= vertical_merge_algorithm_min_rows_to_activate    (stock 16*8192 = 131,072)
          bytes   >= vertical_merge_algorithm_min_bytes_to_activate   (stock 0)
          non-PK cols >= vertical_merge_algorithm_min_columns_to_activate (stock 11)
      A Horizontal merge opens EVERY column stream at once.
    * MergeTreeDataPartWriterOnDisk::Stream — one Wide column stream holds
      2 x max_compress_block_size (plain file buffer + compressor buffer)
      + ~68 KiB of mark buffers. At the stock 1 MiB block size that is
      997 x (2 MiB + 68 KiB) ~= 2.06 GiB of writer buffers for ONE merge,
      plus the read side per source part.
    * MergeTreeDataPartWriterCompact — a Compact part writes ONE shared
      plain file with one CompressedStream PER DISTINCT CODEC
      (`streams_by_codec`), i.e. ~1 stream for a table whose columns all use
      the default codec. 997 buffers become 1.

  So the danger band is exactly {output is Wide} AND {merge is Horizontal}:
  between ~10,330 rows (Wide) and 131,072 rows (Vertical activates). The live
  table was measured INSIDE it — Wide, 13,693 rows, 13.31 MiB uncompressed.

  HOW THE SETTINGS BELOW EMPTY THAT BAND (metric_log, 997 columns):

    min_rows_for_wide_part  = 100,000,000
    min_bytes_for_wide_part = 1,073,741,824 (1 GiB)
        Wide now requires 1e8 rows AND 1 GiB uncompressed. At
        collect_interval_milliseconds = 1000 the table holds at most
        3 d x 86,400 = 259,200 rows and 259,200 x 1,015 B = 250.9 MiB
        uncompressed under the 3-day TTL.
          rows  margin: 100,000,000 / 259,200      = 385.8x
          bytes margin: 1,073,741,824 / 263,088,000 =   4.1x
        Either threshold alone forces Compact (the test is OR), so the
        guarantee is doubly redundant. Every metric_log part is Compact =>
        ~1 write stream => the 997-stream merge cannot exist.

    vertical_merge_algorithm_min_rows_to_activate    = 1
    vertical_merge_algorithm_min_columns_to_activate = 1
    vertical_merge_algorithm_min_bytes_to_activate   = 0
    enable_vertical_merge_algorithm                  = 1
    allow_vertical_merges_from_compact_to_wide_parts = 1
        The second line of defence, and the one that covers a table that is
        ALREADY Wide (see the migration note): with these, Wide implies
        rows >= 1 and non-PK columns >= 1, which every merge satisfies, so
        Wide implies Vertical. Formally the band {Wide AND Horizontal} is the
        set of merges with rows >= min_rows_for_wide_part (1e8) and
        rows < vertical_..._min_rows_to_activate (1) — empty, for every part
        size, independent of the TTL horizon.
        `allow_vertical_merges_from_compact_to_wide_parts` is pinned because
        it gates the Compact->Wide transition merge (MergeTask.cpp:1323) —
        the exact merge that produced the measured 13,693-row Wide part. Its
        24.8 default is already 1; pinned so it stays a contract.

    max_compress_block_size = 65536, min_compress_block_size = 32768
        Bounds the ONE path the vertical settings cannot cover: a TTL merge.
        `need_remove_expired_values` forces Horizontal unconditionally
        (MergeTask.cpp:1314), before the part-type and row-count tests. If a
        legacy Wide part is still present when its rows expire, that merge is
        Wide+Horizontal no matter what. At 64 KiB blocks its writer holds
        997 x (2 x 64 KiB + 68 KiB) ~= 191 MiB instead of ~2.06 GiB — an 11x
        cut that keeps it inside the merge budget rather than 107 % of the
        server cap. Applied to every kept log, since every kept log has a TTL.

    max_bytes_to_merge_at_max_space_in_pool = 134217728 (128 MiB)
        Same clause the netops tables carry (init.sql, 1-2 GiB): the stock
        150 GB means no part is ever too big to re-merge. 128 MiB is ~2x the
        largest 3-day on-disk corpus of any table here, so it never blocks a
        TTL delete merge (a single part can never exceed the cap that built
        it), and it retires an accumulated part from merge selection instead
        of rewriting it forever.

  NOT CHANGED, deliberately:
    * collect_interval_milliseconds stays 1000. 60 s would cut metric_log's
      row rate 60x, but scripts/scale-miniladder.py's ONLY ClickHouse memory
      instrument is max()/countIf() over CurrentMetric_MemoryTracking and
      CurrentMetric_MergesMutationsMemoryTracking in system.metric_log over
      the run window, and the thing it must catch is a ONE-SECOND transient
      (13 of them in a 15-minute window, medians identical to a clean run).
      At 60 s cadence the chance of sampling a given 1 s transient is 1/60:
      the expected catch over s06's window would be 13/60 ~= 0.2 of them. The
      gate would go blind to precisely the failure it exists to detect. The
      cost of 1 s (1,015 B/s = 83.6 MiB/day uncompressed) is now paid by
      Compact merges, which is what made the cadence expensive in the first
      place.
    * flush_interval_milliseconds stays 7500 on every log. Raising it to 30 s
      would cut initial part creation 4x, but the harness reads metric_log
      immediately after the run window WITHOUT issuing SYSTEM FLUSH LOGS
      (verified: no FLUSH LOGS anywhere in scripts/), so a longer interval
      silently drops up to that much of the tail — including a transient in
      the last seconds of the run. Pinned explicitly so the trade-off is a
      visible decision.
    * asynchronous_metrics_update_period_s stays 1 (server default, not set
      here). It is the tick that hard-sets CurrentMetric_MemoryTracking to
      process RSS (MemoryTracker::setRSS); slowing it degrades the same
      1-second peak signal the harness reads.

  part_log CANNOT see any of this, and no setting changes that.
  Context::getPartLog (24.8 Interpreters/Context.cpp) returns {} when
  `part_database == DatabaseCatalog::SYSTEM_DATABASE`, hard-coded, to avoid an
  infinite logging cycle. Every merge of a system.* table is therefore absent
  from system.part_log — including moving part_log to another database, since
  the test is on the SOURCE table's database. Attribution for system-table
  merges comes from metric_log's ProfileEvent_MergedColumns / _MergedRows and
  from trace_log (below), never from part_log. This is stated so the next
  investigation does not re-derive it as a "contradiction".
  ==========================================================================

  trace_log + asynchronous_metric_log ADDED 2026-08-29 (same brief, section 0
  and 6(d)). Both were missing, which is why the 2.4-2.9 GiB was attributed by
  arithmetic instead of measured:
    * asynchronous_metric_log — the ONLY history of MemoryResident, jemalloc.*
      (retained was 21.65 GiB live), MarkCacheBytes, PrimaryKeyBytes and
      TotalPartsOfMergeTreeTables. Without it, candidate 2 (jemalloc dirty-page
      retention) is unquantifiable. Volume: N async metrics x 1 Hz; on 24.8
      with cgroup + jemalloc + per-disk gauges N is ~300-500, i.e. 26-43 M
      rows/day at ~20 B/row uncompressed, which LowCardinality metric names
      and a Float64 value compress ~15-30x -> roughly 40-110 MB/day on disk,
      ~120-330 MB under the 3-day TTL. That is the largest of the kept logs
      and it is bounded and deliberate.
    * trace_log — the sink the memory profiler never had. The stock image
      already sets total_memory_profiler_step = 4194304 (see memory.xml,
      where it is now pinned), which emits a stack trace with
      trace_type = 'Memory' every 4 MiB the GLOBAL tracker grows, from
      background threads too. The s06 peak ramp (96 -> 3,895 MiB in 4 s) is
      ~950 such rows: bounded, and it names the allocator directly:
        SELECT arrayStringConcat(arrayMap(x -> demangle(addressToSymbol(x)),
                                          trace), '\n') AS stack, sum(size)
        FROM system.trace_log
        WHERE event_time BETWEEN ... AND trace_type IN ('Memory','MemoryPeak')
        GROUP BY stack ORDER BY 2 DESC LIMIT 20
      total_memory_tracker_sample_probability stays 0 (memory.xml): it is a
      per-allocation probability, i.e. unbounded in principle, and the free
      step profiler above already answers the question. Turn it (and the
      per-profile memory_profiler_sample_probability) to 0.01 for ONE
      diagnostic run only, in users.d, if a stack-level histogram is needed.
      VOLUME WARNING: creating trace_log also switches ON the per-thread query
      profiler (ThreadStatus only starts it when a trace collector exists), at
      query_profiler_real_time_period_ns / _cpu_time_period_ns = 1e9, i.e.
      ~1 row/s per active query thread. That, not the memory profiler, is
      trace_log's dominant writer under load. It is bounded by the 1-day TTL;
      to cut it further set both periods to 0 in a users.d profile.

  ==========================================================================
  WHAT TAKES EFFECT ONLY AFTER A CLICKHOUSE RESTART

  <ttl>, <settings> and <collect_interval_milliseconds> are all applied when
  the table is CREATED — nothing here is hot-reloadable, and an ALTER TABLE
  MODIFY SETTING on system.metric_log would NOT be enough either (see "Wide is
  sticky" below). Concretely, on the next `docker compose up -d clickhouse`:

    1. SystemLog::prepareTable (24.8) renders the CREATE statement it would
       issue and compares it to the live one. Every kept log below now differs
       (new SETTINGS clause), so ClickHouse RENAMES each existing table to
       `<name>_0` (`_1`, ... if taken) and creates a fresh one. This is
       automatic; no operator action is needed for NEW rows to land in a
       correctly-configured table.
    2. The renamed tables keep their disk and their OLD engine definition.
       Drop them — this history is DISPOSABLE: metric_log is a 3-day rolling
       perf trace, part_log a 3-day merge trace, and the s06 run they cover is
       already written up in docs/scale/. Nothing downstream reads them.
    3. DROPPING system.metric_log IS REQUIRED, not cosmetic. The merged part
       type is min(all source part types, choosePartFormat(...)) and Wide sorts
       BEFORE Compact in MergeTreeDataPartType (FutureMergedMutatedPart::assign),
       so WIDE IS STICKY: the live table already holds a Wide part (measured:
       Wide, 13,693 rows), and every merge that touches it produces another
       Wide part no matter what min_rows_for_wide_part says. Left in place, the
       old table would keep its old engine anyway (it was renamed), so this is
       really about not paying for it twice — but if an operator instead tries
       to fix the LIVE table with ALTER ... MODIFY SETTING, the sticky-Wide rule
       is why that does not work until the existing Wide parts age out under
       the TTL. Recreate, do not patch.

  Run once, after the restart:
    docker exec netops-clickhouse-1 clickhouse-client -q "
      DROP TABLE IF EXISTS system.text_log; DROP TABLE IF EXISTS system.trace_log_0;
      DROP TABLE IF EXISTS system.processors_profile_log;
      DROP TABLE IF EXISTS system.query_views_log;
      DROP TABLE IF EXISTS system.metric_log_0; DROP TABLE IF EXISTS system.query_log_0;
      DROP TABLE IF EXISTS system.part_log_0;   DROP TABLE IF EXISTS system.error_log_0;
      DROP TABLE IF EXISTS system.asynchronous_metric_log_0;"
  then confirm the new engines actually carry the settings:
    docker exec netops-clickhouse-1 clickhouse-client -q "
      SELECT name, engine_full FROM system.tables
       WHERE database='system' AND name IN
             ('metric_log','query_log','part_log','error_log',
              'asynchronous_metric_log','trace_log') FORMAT Vertical"
  A table whose engine_full has no SETTINGS clause was created BEFORE this
  file was mounted; a table that is MISSING entirely means its <settings>
  string failed to parse and the CREATE was rejected — check the server log,
  because that failure is otherwise silent.
  (The same one-time drop is documented in
  docs/runbooks/storage-and-volume-operations.md.)
  ==========================================================================
-->
<clickhouse>
    <text_log remove="1"/>
    <processors_profile_log remove="1"/>
    <query_thread_log remove="1"/>
    <query_views_log remove="1"/>
    <opentelemetry_span_log remove="1"/>
    <session_log remove="1"/>

    <query_log>
        <ttl>event_date + INTERVAL 7 DAY DELETE</ttl>
        <flush_interval_milliseconds>7500</flush_interval_milliseconds>
        <settings>enable_vertical_merge_algorithm = 1, vertical_merge_algorithm_min_rows_to_activate = 1, vertical_merge_algorithm_min_bytes_to_activate = 0, vertical_merge_algorithm_min_columns_to_activate = 1, allow_vertical_merges_from_compact_to_wide_parts = 1, max_compress_block_size = 65536, min_compress_block_size = 32768, max_bytes_to_merge_at_max_space_in_pool = 134217728</settings>
    </query_log>

    <!-- The 997-column table the P2/s06 brief is about. It carries the two
         wide-part thresholds ON TOP of the shared block: those are what make
         "Wide" unreachable, and the shared vertical settings are what make
         "Wide implies Vertical" if it ever were reached. -->
    <metric_log>
        <ttl>event_date + INTERVAL 3 DAY DELETE</ttl>
        <collect_interval_milliseconds>1000</collect_interval_milliseconds>
        <flush_interval_milliseconds>7500</flush_interval_milliseconds>
        <settings>min_rows_for_wide_part = 100000000, min_bytes_for_wide_part = 1073741824, enable_vertical_merge_algorithm = 1, vertical_merge_algorithm_min_rows_to_activate = 1, vertical_merge_algorithm_min_bytes_to_activate = 0, vertical_merge_algorithm_min_columns_to_activate = 1, allow_vertical_merges_from_compact_to_wide_parts = 1, max_compress_block_size = 65536, min_compress_block_size = 32768, max_bytes_to_merge_at_max_space_in_pool = 134217728</settings>
    </metric_log>

    <!-- ENABLED BY THE STOCK 24.8 IMAGE, and until now unbounded here: the
         #96a TTL rule only ever covered the logs this file names. system.error_log
         is the ONLY place 15 of the 17 s06 MEMORY_LIMIT_EXCEEDED events were
         recorded (query_log saw 2; the rest were raised on background threads),
         so it is the customer-visible fact the harness is meant to gate on
         (brief section 6(e)) — it is kept deliberately, with the same 7-day
         horizon as query_log, not left to drift. -->
    <error_log>
        <ttl>event_date + INTERVAL 7 DAY DELETE</ttl>
        <collect_interval_milliseconds>1000</collect_interval_milliseconds>
        <flush_interval_milliseconds>7500</flush_interval_milliseconds>
        <settings>enable_vertical_merge_algorithm = 1, vertical_merge_algorithm_min_rows_to_activate = 1, vertical_merge_algorithm_min_bytes_to_activate = 0, vertical_merge_algorithm_min_columns_to_activate = 1, allow_vertical_merges_from_compact_to_wide_parts = 1, max_compress_block_size = 65536, min_compress_block_size = 32768, max_bytes_to_merge_at_max_space_in_pool = 134217728</settings>
    </error_log>

    <!-- P2: merge/part accounting, the instrument the merge-budget change is
         measured with. Same 3-day horizon as metric_log — the two are read
         together (metric_log gives the memory curve, part_log gives the merge
         that caused it, for every database EXCEPT system). -->
    <part_log>
        <ttl>event_date + INTERVAL 3 DAY DELETE</ttl>
        <flush_interval_milliseconds>7500</flush_interval_milliseconds>
        <settings>enable_vertical_merge_algorithm = 1, vertical_merge_algorithm_min_rows_to_activate = 1, vertical_merge_algorithm_min_bytes_to_activate = 0, vertical_merge_algorithm_min_columns_to_activate = 1, allow_vertical_merges_from_compact_to_wide_parts = 1, max_compress_block_size = 65536, min_compress_block_size = 32768, max_bytes_to_merge_at_max_space_in_pool = 134217728</settings>
    </part_log>

    <!-- P2/s06 section 0: absent, so no MemoryResident / jemalloc.* /
         MarkCacheBytes / TotalParts* history existed to decompose the peak
         with. 3 days, same horizon as metric_log so the two join. -->
    <asynchronous_metric_log>
        <ttl>event_date + INTERVAL 3 DAY DELETE</ttl>
        <flush_interval_milliseconds>7500</flush_interval_milliseconds>
        <settings>enable_vertical_merge_algorithm = 1, vertical_merge_algorithm_min_rows_to_activate = 1, vertical_merge_algorithm_min_bytes_to_activate = 0, vertical_merge_algorithm_min_columns_to_activate = 1, allow_vertical_merges_from_compact_to_wide_parts = 1, max_compress_block_size = 65536, min_compress_block_size = 32768, max_bytes_to_merge_at_max_space_in_pool = 134217728</settings>
    </asynchronous_metric_log>

    <!-- P2/s06 section 6(d): the memory profiler's missing sink. 1 day — the
         shortest horizon here, because the per-thread query profiler that
         switches on with it is trace_log's dominant writer under load. -->
    <trace_log>
        <ttl>event_date + INTERVAL 1 DAY DELETE</ttl>
        <flush_interval_milliseconds>7500</flush_interval_milliseconds>
        <settings>enable_vertical_merge_algorithm = 1, vertical_merge_algorithm_min_rows_to_activate = 1, vertical_merge_algorithm_min_bytes_to_activate = 0, vertical_merge_algorithm_min_columns_to_activate = 1, allow_vertical_merges_from_compact_to_wide_parts = 1, max_compress_block_size = 65536, min_compress_block_size = 32768, max_bytes_to_merge_at_max_space_in_pool = 134217728</settings>
    </trace_log>
</clickhouse>
